<item>
  <title>ReDiLEEP: a doctoral network on response diversity</title>
  <dc:creator>Owen Petchey</dc:creator>
  <link>https://predictive-ecology-zurich.org/posts/organising/2026-09-05-redileep/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p><a href="https://redileep.eu/">ReDiLEEP</a> — “using <strong>Re</strong>sponse <strong>Di</strong>versity knowledge to <strong>L</strong>everage <strong>E</strong>fficient <strong>E</strong>cosystem <strong>P</strong>reservation and restoration” — is a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions (MSCA) Doctoral Network funded through the EU’s Horizon Europe programme.</p>
<p>The network recruits PhD candidates into linked projects across a consortium of universities, research institutes, policy organisations, NGOs, and industry partners. Doctoral researchers get disciplinary training, cross-sector supervision, secondments to partner organisations, and a shared cohort experience across the whole network, rather than working in isolation at a single institution.</p>
<p>ReDiLEEP’s three interconnected goals are:</p>
<ul>
<li>developing standardised metrics for measuring response diversity across ecosystems;</li>
<li>fostering collaboration between ecological research and policy implementation; and</li>
<li>creating practical frameworks that put response diversity to work in conservation and restoration.</li>
</ul>
<p>Response diversity — variation among species or organisms in how they respond to environmental change — is a big part of what keeps ecosystem functions stable when conditions shift. Helping turn that idea into something measurable and usable by policy- and decision-makers is exactly the kind of applied, cross-institutional organising this group is glad to be part of.</p>
<p><em>(Details of our specific role and doctoral projects within the consortium to be added.)</em></p>



 ]]></description>
