<item>
  <title>The Response Diversity Network</title>
  <dc:creator>Owen Petchey</dc:creator>
  <link>https://predictive-ecology-zurich.org/posts/organising/2026-09-05-response-diversity-network/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>The <a href="https://responsediversitynetwork.github.io/RDN-website/">Response Diversity Network</a> is a community of researchers working on a deceptively simple question: what is response diversity, why does it matter, and what should the field prioritise next?</p>
<p>Response diversity — the idea that different species or organisms respond differently to the same environmental change — is increasingly framed as a sustainability strategy: it’s part of why ecosystem services can keep being delivered even as conditions shift, since not everything fails at once.</p>
<p>The network runs regular seminars with researchers presenting on biodiversity–stability relationships, species traits, and ecosystem resilience; maintains a shared Zotero library of response-diversity-related papers; organises symposia at conferences including GfO, BES, and ESJ; and sends out a quarterly newsletter. A steering committee has been in place since November 2022, with a new leadership team as of this year.</p>
<p>This group contributes to the network’s work on turning response diversity research into concrete opportunities for policy- and decision-makers — one strand of the broader push to make response diversity a genuinely useful concept outside academic ecology, not just inside it.</p>



 ]]></description>
