<item>
  <title>A tool for valuing contributions in teams</title>
  <dc:creator>Owen Petchey</dc:creator>
  <link>https://predictive-ecology-zurich.org/posts/organising/2019-03-13-valuing-contributions-in-teams/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>Working in teams can be some of the most stimulating, exciting, and important experiences we have. At other times things can get a bit sticky — for example when it comes to attributing rewards, such as deciding authorship and authorship order on publications. Proactive planning and assessment of contributions can help, alongside a collaboration agreement.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://owenpetchey.shinyapps.io/valuing_contributions_in_teams/">web application for valuing contributions in teams</a> summarises the contributions a team can report in the associated <a href="https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1jw3rfSDpQbT-IfgGsCbYSy7XIe92UiZqCFw8zMZcLRM">Google Sheet</a>. Below is a bit more detail about the problems it addresses and how.</p>
<section id="in-the-google-sheet-you" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="in-the-google-sheet-you">In the Google Sheet, you:</h3>
<ul>
<li>Enter the project information you have so far; update as required.</li>
<li>Enter your team members by name.</li>
<li>Enter the contributions made by each team member on the contributions sheet.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section id="problems-and-solutions-in-team-projects" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="problems-and-solutions-in-team-projects">Problems and solutions in team projects</h3>
<p>There are a number of recurring challenges:</p>
<ul>
<li>Authorship order alone doesn’t capture what are, in reality, nuanced, continuous, and diverse types of contribution.</li>
<li>Lack of transparency in team members’ efforts can lead to <a href="https://www.thecut.com/2017/04/what-to-do-when-you-feel-like-the-only-roommate-who-cleans.html">overestimating our own contribution and underestimating others’</a>.</li>
<li>Contributions change over the course of a project.</li>
<li>Actual and intended contributions can diverge.</li>
<li>Value created by synergies is hard to account for.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some solutions to these problems:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use the Contributor Roles Taxonomy (CRediT) with a degree of contribution (lead, equal, supporting), and report this in publications.</li>
<li>Keep a team-accessible list of contributions.</li>
<li>Track how contributions evolve, from intended to actual/final.</li>
<li>Use a transparent, flexible method for translating actual/final contributions into authorship order.</li>
<li>Give each contributor responsibility for recording their own contributions.</li>
<li>Give the project leader responsibility for making sure this self-responsibility is understood, remembered, and acted upon — e.g.&nbsp;by building it into the team’s research culture, and issuing reminders.</li>
</ul>


</section>

 ]]></description>
